# Inputs for ./bootstrap.sh — copy to .env.bootstrap and fill in.
#
# .gitignore excludes .env.* so .env.bootstrap cannot be committed. This
# template is the only version that belongs in git; never put a real value here.
#
# See docs/BOOTSTRAP.md for what each of these unlocks and what is derived from
# them. Everything not listed below is derived: the AWS region, the AWS account
# ID, the Round 5 app principal ARN, the manifest path, the Databricks CLI
# profile, the SQL warehouse and the Databricks App service principal.
#
# bootstrap.sh also provisions .venv (uv sync --locked) and frontend/dist
# (npm ci && npm run build) before it provisions anything billed. Both are
# idempotent; --skip-install turns them off.

# --- Databricks: a workspace service principal's OAuth secret (M2M) ---------
# Not a personal access token, and not `databricks auth login` state.
DATABRICKS_HOST=https://dbc-00000000-0000.cloud.databricks.com
DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID=replace-with-service-principal-client-id
DATABRICKS_CLIENT_SECRET=replace-at-runtime-never-commit

# --- AWS: a principal carrying policies 1-3 in docs/iam/ --------------------
# The fourth policy there is only for the opt-in S3 state backend. A default
# install needs no S3 access at all.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=replace-at-runtime-never-commit
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=replace-at-runtime-never-commit
# Optional. Left unset, bootstrap takes the region the AWS CLI is already
# configured with (`aws configure get region`) and says so; it prompts only when
# there is none to find.
# AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-west-2
# Only for temporary credentials. Leave unset for a permanent IAM user; a stale
# value inherited from an SSO session is cleared rather than used.
# AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=

# --- Optional, for when derivation cannot be unambiguous ---------------------
# Ownership tag on every AWS resource. Defaults to the service principal's own
# identity, which is a UUID; an email here is what puts a human name ringside.
# ANTI_DEMO_OWNER=you@example.com

# Required only when the workspace has more than one SQL warehouse. Bootstrap
# lists them and refuses to guess, because Round 4 seals this into its contract.
# DATABRICKS_WAREHOUSE_ID=

# The Databricks App to create or adopt. Its service principal client ID is
# sealed into the Round 4 contract, so the app must exist before setup runs.
# DATABRICKS_APP_NAME=lakebase-anti-demo
# DATABRICKS_APP_CLIENT_ID=

# Required only when the AWS identity is a temporary assumed role, because the
# STS ARN drops the IAM path. Bind the stable IAM role or user ARN, never the
# arn:aws:sts::...:assumed-role/... form.
# ROUND5_APP_PRINCIPAL_ARN=

# Round 6's Delta destination catalog. Defaults to main.
# DATABRICKS_CDF_CATALOG=main

# Round 4's Delta source catalog. It must already exist and this principal must
# be able to create schemas in it. Defaults to main, the catalog Databricks
# creates for a Unity Catalog-enabled workspace, so usually there is nothing to
# set; if main is absent or invisible the installer refuses early and names this
# variable. Read only on a first provision: after that the manifest's sealed
# value wins, and a value here that disagrees with the seal is refused rather
# than silently ignored.
# ROUND4_CATALOG=main

# Override the generation whose manifest this run operates on. Bootstrap picks
# the highest-numbered existing .anti-demo-v*/manifest.json, or creates
# .anti-demo-v7. Adopting an existing generation means --apply reconciles the
# LIVE installation rather than making a new one: terraform apply over it, both
# database lanes reset, Round 3 anchors cleared. Pass --new-generation to build
# a separate installation instead -- a second fleet and a second bill.
# ANTI_DEMO_MANIFEST=

# Expiry for a first provision, in hours. Default 72. Advisory only: nothing
# reaps on it, and only `antidemo cleanup --yes` stops the spend.
# ANTI_DEMO_TTL_HOURS=72

# --- Optional: where the deployed app reads its seal from -------------------
# The Databricks secret scope `./bootstrap.sh --deploy-only` writes the sealed
# manifest into. Defaults to lakebase-anti-demo-<generation directory>, which is
# stable across resets. If the app is already bound to a different scope,
# bootstrap adopts that one rather than orphaning it.
# ANTI_DEMO_SECRET_SCOPE=
#
# ANTI_DEMO_MANIFEST_JSON is deliberately absent. It is the one bootstrap
# variable an operator never sets: app.yaml binds it inside the deployed app to
# the *contents* of the secret above, so it exists only in the app's own
# environment. Setting it here would do nothing.

# --- Optional: Terraform state in S3 — OPT-IN, NEW INSTALLATIONS ONLY -------
# The _terraform_init patch this needs has landed in server/lifecycle.py, so
# bootstrap.sh accepts `--state-backend s3`. What that buys is the path being
# open, not the path being exercised: no `terraform init` has ever been run
# against the generated backend override. Treat it as unproven.
#
# The default, and the only state any existing installation has, is a local file
# in the generation directory beside the manifest. Leaving these unset changes
# nothing.
#
# s3 is accepted for NEW installations only — switching a live one is
# `terraform init -migrate-state`, done by hand, and docs/DEPLOY.md has the
# procedure. It also requires Terraform 1.11+ for S3-native locking, and the
# fourth IAM policy in docs/iam/.
# ANTI_DEMO_TF_BACKEND=local
# ANTI_DEMO_TF_STATE_BUCKET=
# Defaults to anti-demo/<generation directory>/terraform.tfstate, keyed on the
# generation rather than on run_id, because run_id changes on every reset.
# ANTI_DEMO_TF_STATE_KEY=
